INT. SUPERMAN - CONTINUOUS

MALONE
D'y' think they'll be able to get that done before I buy the house--or rather the abbey? They're both abbeys.

putting that aside rather impatiently.

VIOLET
Oh, well, let us talk sense, Mr Malone. You must feel that we haven't been talking sense so far.

MALONE
I can't say I do. I mean all I say.

VIOLET
Then you don't know Hector as I do. He is romantic and faddy--he gets it from you, I fancy--and he wants a certain sort of wife to take care of him. Not a faddy sort of person, you know.

MALONE
Somebody like you, perhaps?

quietly.

VIOLET
Well, yes. But you cannot very well ask me to undertake this with absolutely no means of keeping up his position.

alarmed.

MALONE
Stop a bit, stop a bit. Where are we getting to? I'm not aware that I'm asking you to undertake anything.

VIOLET
Of course, Mr Malone, you can make it very difficult for me to speak to you if you choose to misunderstand me.

half bewildered.

MALONE
I don't wish to take any unfair advantage; but we seem to have got off the straight track somehow.

Straker, with the air of a man who has been making haste, opens the little gate, and admits Hector, who, snorting with indignation, comes upon the lawn, and is making for his father when Violet, greatly dismayed, springs up and intercepts him. Straker doer not wait; at least he does not remain visibly within earshot.

VIOLET
Oh, how unlucky! Now please, Hector, say nothing. Go away until I have finished speaking to your father.

inexorably.

HECTOR
No, Violet: I mean to have this thing out, right away.

He puts her aside; passes her by; and faces his father, whose cheeks darken as his Irish blood begins to simmer.

HECTOR
Dad: you've not played this hand straight.

MALONE
Hwat d'y'mean?

HECTOR
You've opened a letter addressed to me. You've impersonated me and stolen a march on this lady. That's dishonorable.

threateningly.

MALONE
Now you take care what you're saying, Hector. Take care, I tell you.

HECTOR
I have taken care. I am taking care. I'm taking care of my honor and my position in English society.

hotly.

MALONE
Your position has been got by my money: do you know that?

HECTOR
Well, you've just spoiled it all by opening that letter. A letter from an English lady, not addressed to you--a confidential letter! a delicate letter! a private letter opened by my father! That's a sort of thing a man can't struggle against in England. The sooner we go back together the better.

He appeals mutely to the heavens to witness the shame and anguish of two outcasts.

snubbing him with an instinctive dislike for scene making.

VIOLET
Don't be unreasonable, Hector. It was quite natural of Mr Malone to open my letter: his name was on the envelope.

MALONE
There! You've no common sense, Hector. I thank you, Miss Robinson.

HECTOR
I thank you, too. It's very kind of you. My father knows no better.

furiously clenching his fists.

MALONE
Hector--

with undaunted moral force.

HECTOR
Oh, it's no use hectoring me. A private letter's a private letter, dad: you can't get over that.

MALONE [raising his voice] I won't be talked back to by you, d'y' hear?

VIOLET
Ssh! please, please. Here they all come.

Father and son, checked, glare mutely at one another as Tanner comes in through the little gate with Ramsden, followed by Octavius and Ann.

VIOLET
Back already!

TANNER
The Alhambra is not open this afternoon.

VIOLET
What a sell!

Tanner passes on, and presently finds himself between Hector and a strange elder, both apparently on the verge of personal combat. He looks from one to the other for an explanation. They sulkily avoid his eye, and nurse their wrath in silence.

RAMSDEN
Is it wise for you to be out in the sunshine with such a headache, Violet?

TANNER
Have you recovered too, Malone?
